5.1  What a GPU does differently

TL;DR
  - A CPU has a few powerful cores, built to run one thread fast, branches and
    all. A GPU has thousands of simple ones, built to run the same instructions
    on huge amounts of data at once.
  - That fits pixels, video and the matrix maths of machine learning; it doesn't
    fit branchy, step-by-step code.
  - A discrete GPU has its own memory (VRAM), very fast but separate: data must
    be copied there over PCIe. An integrated GPU shares the system's RAM.

      lspci -k | grep -A3 -E 'VGA|3D|Display'   # GPUs and their drivers

On your machine
  card0     Intel, driver i915, shows the boot screen
  Screens   eDP-1 1920x1200 · free: DP-1, HDMI-A-1
  Compute   /dev/dri/renderD128

Pitfalls
  - Copying data between RAM and VRAM is often the bottleneck: a GPU only pays
    off when it does a lot of work per byte copied.
  - Running out of VRAM fails or slows down hard; it isn't extended by swap the
    way RAM is.
  - Laptops with two GPUs run most programs on the integrated one; the discrete
    one has to be requested (prime-run, or the desktop's "run with dedicated
    GPU" option).

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 5.1 --why

